oints is not performed in the valuation path.\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1 \u2014 Macro variable check (T0, all external):\n- 10-Year Treasury: 4.70% [Actual \u2014 YCharts / Trading Economics, 2026-08-21/24]\n- Real 10Y rate: ~1.3% (4.70% minus CPI 3.4%) [Arithmetic]\n- Fed funds target: 3.50\u20133.75%, on hold for 5th consecutive meeting (July 2026);\n  market prices ~1/3 probability of a hike [Actual \u2014 Trading Economics, 2026-07]\n- Dollar (DXY): [Unconfirmed \u2014 search budget exhausted; not incorporated]\n- CPI: 3.4% y/y July 2026, down from 3.5% June [Actual \u2014 BLS via Trading Economics]\n- IG credit spreads (BofA OAS): 0.81% [Actual \u2014 FRED ICE BofA, 2026-08-24]\n- HY credit spreads: ~2.75% [Estimate \u2014 ts2.tech, 2026-08-20]\n- Liquidity stance: neutral-to-tightening bias; low spreads, rising rate risk\n- Cycle position: late expansion with nascent stagflation risk\n\nStep 1-2 \u2014 Regime probability distribution:\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 45%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 30%\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 12%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 13%\nRationale: 10Y near 4.7%, CPI 3.4%, Fed on hold with hike risk, IG spreads at\ncyclical lows. The distribution is dominated by a reflation/stagflation mix.\n\nStep 1-3 \u2014 Macro sensitivity matrix (\u22123 to +3):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: \u22121 (core customer is low/mid-income; credit stress)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (strong USD lowers imported apparel cost)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (tight wallets push trade-down toward off-price)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: +1 (counter-cyclical demand; 2008/2020 evidence)\n- Commodity sensitivity: 0 (apparel; energy only indirect)\n\nStep 1-4 \u2014 Macro alignment:\nExpected macro impact = 0.45\u00d7(+0.4) + 0.30\u00d7(+0.4) + 0.12\u00d7(+0.2) + 0.13\u00d7(+0.2)\n\u2248 +0.3 \u2192 NEUTRAL. Trade-down benefits roughly offset high-rate consumer stress.\nNo conviction adjustment from macro.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1 \u2014 Relevant base rates:\n- Large-cap quality compounder, 5-year survival: >95% [Base rate \u2014 Assumption]\n- Large-cap consumer at >30x normalized earnings producing 5-year excess return:\n  ~20-30% [Base rate \u2014 high-multiple mean reversion]\n- Off-price retailer gaining share in consumer downturns: ~80% (2008-09, 2020)\n  [Estimate \u2014 historical evidence]\n- Long-term store-expansion target (2,400 \u2192 2,900 Ross + 700 dd's) achieved in\n  full: ~60-70% [Base rate \u2014 Assumption]\n\nStep 1.5-2 \u2014 Deviation assessment:\nMy base case (mid-single-digit growth, 14% op margin, IV $162) is NOT above base\nrate. The deviation lies with the market: at $239.04, the market is pricing a\nbullish outcome (>$280 scenario) at roughly 50-60% probability \u2014 more than 2\u00d7\nthe base rate of a >30x multiple compounder delivering. I therefore hold to\nbase-rate discipline. Company-specific justifications for a modest quality\npremium (30-year off-price track record, fortress net cash $3.08B, 39% ROE,\n103% FCF conversion) are valid and are already embedded in my margins and WACC.\n\nStep 1.5-3 \u2014 Base-rate shock check:\nMarket-implied success probability > 2\u00d7 base rate \u2192 RED FLAG on the price,\nnot on the business. This is a necessary input to the bear/bull weighting below.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1 \u2014 Business understanding:\nRoss Stores operates ~1,900 off-price apparel and home-goods stores (Ross Dress\nfor Less + dd's DISCOUNTS), buying opportunistic brand-name inventory from\ndepartment stores and vendors at deep discounts and selling 20-60% below regular\nretail. Its model monetizes retail industry excess inventory and consumer\ntrade-down demand. Understandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2 \u2014 Moat assessment (0-3 each) and direction:\n- Intangible assets (brand): 2 \u2014 strong value brand; not a premium brand. Stable\n- Switching costs: 1 \u2014 retail, low frictions. Stable\n- Network effects: 1 \u2014 store density aids buying, but not a user network. Stable\n- Cost advantage: 3 \u2014 off-price buying model is structurally advantaged. Widening\n- Economies of scale: 3 \u2014 #2 domestic off-price scale; DC network. Widening\n- Regulatory barriers: 1 \u2014 minimal. Stable\n- Data moat: 1 \u2014 modest CRM/marketing data. Stable\nTotal: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: STABLE, with modest widening in cost\nadvantage/share gains (Q1 +21%, Q2 +10% comps while TJX grew ~4% [Estimate \u2014\nMotley Fool, 2026-08-22]).\n\nStep 2-3 \u2014 Lynch classification:\nFAST GROWER currently: Q1 total sales +21% and Q2 comp +10% [Actual]. Historical\nframe is Stalwart/compounder. Key checkpoint: the current double-digit comps are\npartly inflated by tax-refund timing and tariff dynamics; laps get harder in H2\nFY2027 (Q3 base +10.4%, Q4 base +12.2%). Growth must be down-weighted beyond FY2027.\n\nStep 2-4 \u2014 Financial strength scorecard (FY2026 unless noted):\n- Revenue CAGR: 3-yr +6.8% [Arithmetic]; 5-yr +12.7% [Pack, Arithmetic]\n- Operating margin: 11.9% FY2026; 4-yr median 11.6% [Arithmetic]\n- EPS: FY2026 $6.61 vs FY2023 $4.38 \u2192 3-yr CAGR +14.7% [Arithmetic]\n- ROE: 38.98% [Aggregator]; ROIC \u2248 27-30% [Estimate]\n- FCF margin: $2.208B/$22.751B = 9.7%; FCF/NI = 103% [Arithmetic]\n- Net cash: $4.594B \u2212 $1.018B LT debt \u2212 $0.500B current portion = $3.076B\n  [Arithmetic, using debt components specified in Section 3]\n- Interest expense FY2026: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not in pack]; irrelevant given net cash\nJudgment: EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5 \u2014 Capital allocation scorecard (total /15):\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 net issuance \u22122.0%/yr, but repurchases executed at\n   ~30x earnings are value-destructive at the margin.\n2. M&A track record: 3/3 \u2014 no material M&A; no impairment risk.\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 115 new stores at ~$3.5M/store; incremental ROIC\n   historically >20% [Estimate].\n4. Rationality of mix: 3/3 \u2014 expansion + dividend (yield 0.73%) + buyback.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 Q1/Q2 FY2027 beat; FY guidance raised twice.\nInsider: CEO/mgmt <5%; no insider buying cluster \u2192 0 bonus.\nTOTAL: 13/15 \u2014 strong allocator, minor buyback-timing demerit.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1 \u2014 R&