clical (off-price), not\n  hard-cyclical; no platform/optionality component; no binary regulatory path.\n- Why Engine 1? DCF + multiples is appropriate; Engine 2 (cycle-adjusted) is\n  unnecessary because earnings variability is low (except COVID shock); Engine\n  3/4/5 are inapplicable.\n- Transition check: Not in transition. Revenue 2021 COVID dip was a temporary\n  exogenous shock, not an archetype shift. Basis-change check: FY2020 operating\n  lease appearance ($3.175B) reflects ASC 842 adoption [Pack warning], not a\n  business event; FY2021 revenue drop is real COVID history (NI $0.085B).\n\nStep 3-1 \u2014 Explicit forecast (years = change of fiscal year, ending each Jan):\n\n| FY | Revenue | Growth | OpM | OI | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | SBC | Owner FCF |\n|----|---------|--------|-----|-----|-------|-----|-------|-----|-----|-----------|\n| 2026A | 22.751 | 7.7% | 11.9% | 2.707 | 2.098 | 0.509 | 0.819 | \u22120.064 | 0.175 | 2.033 |\n| 2027E | 25.30 | +11.2% | 13.5% | 3.416 | 2.647 | 0.550 | 0.950 | \u22120.040 | 0.190 | 2.097 |\n| 2028E | 27.20 | +7.5% | 13.7% | 3.726 | 2.888 | 0.580 | 1.020 | \u22120.050 | 0.200 | 2.298 |\n| 2029E | 29.10 | +7.0% | 13.9% | 4.045 | 3.135 | 0.620 | 1.090 | \u22120.050 | 0.210 | 2.505 |\n| 2030E | 30.90 | +6.2% | 14.0% | 4.326 | 3.353 | 0.660 | 1.150 | \u22120.040 | 0.220 | 2.683 |\n| 2031E | 32.60 | +5.5% | 14.0% | 4.564 | 3.537 | 0.700 | 1.210 | \u22120.040 | 0.230 | 2.837 |\n| 2032E | 34.20 | +4.9% | 14.0% | 4.788 | 3.711 | 0.740 | 1.260 | \u22120.040 | 0.240 | 2.991 |\n| 2033E | 35.70 | +4.4% | 14.0% | 4.998 | 3.873 | 0.780 | 1.300 | \u22120.040 | 0.250 | 3.143 |\n\nForecast driver sources:\n- Revenue FY2027: Q1 actual $6.010B [Filed]; Q2 ~$6.28B [Estimate \u2014 +10% comp\n  [Actual, company] + ~3.5% new stores]; H2 comps assumed +5-7% as laps toughen\n  [Assumption]; total $25.3B (+11.2%) [Estimate].\n- Revenue FY2028-33: company long-term target of 2,900 Ross + 700 dd's vs ~1,900\n  today [Actual \u2014 company via Retail Dive] implies ~5-6%/yr unit growth. Comps\n  fade from high-single to ~2-3% [Assumption]. Guidance beyond FY2027 is NOT\n  provided [Unconfirmed] \u2014 the deceleration path is my assumption, sourced from\n  the store-limit math.\n- Op margin: Q2 FY2027 ex-refund op margin 13.55% (17.6% reported minus 405bps\n  refund) [Estimate \u2014 company via 24/7 Wall St.]. Path to 14.0% by FY2029\n  [Assumption]; tariffs could subtract 50-150bps in adverse cases.\n- Capex: $0.819B FY2026 [Filed]; 115 openings FY2027 [Actual \u2014 company via\n  Retail Dive]; ~$3.5-4.0M per store [Assumption] plus maintenance \u2192 $0.95B.\n- \u0394WC: small source each year, consistent with FY2025-26 pattern [Assumption].\n- Tax: 22.5% [Assumption \u2014 pack withholds effective tax rate; Ross's historical\n  range 22-24%].\n- SBC: grows ~5%/yr [Assumption].\n\nStep 3-2 \u2014 WACC derivation:\n- Risk-free: 4.70% [Actual \u2014 10Y, 2026-08-24]\n- ERP: 4.5% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran-style medium-term equity premium]\n- Beta: 0.877 [Aggregator \u2014 vendor does not state window; limitation noted]\n- Cost of equity: 4.70 + 0.877 \u00d7 4.5 = 8.65%\n- Pre-tax cost of debt: 4.70 + 0.81 (IG OAS) \u2248 5.5% [Estimate]; after-tax 4.26%\n- Weights: E $76.740B; D $1.518B (LT $1.018B + current portion $0.500B\n  [Filed]; leases excluded from capital weights by design); EV $78.258B\n- WACC = 0.981 \u00d7 8.65% + 0.019 \u00d7 4.26% = 8.57% \u2248 8.6%\n\nStep 3-3 \u2014 DCF:\nDiscount factors @8.6% (2027-2033): 1.086, 1.179, 1.281, 1.391, 1.511, 1.641, 1.782\nPV of FCFs: 1.93, 1.95, 1.96, 1.93, 1.88, 1.82, 1.76 \u2192 \u03a3 = 13.24\nTerminal value = 3.143 \u00d7 1.035 / (0.086 \u2212 0.035) = $63.7B [Terminal growth 3.5%\n= Assumption; \u2264 long-run nominal GDP]\nPV of terminal = $63.7 / 1.782 = 35.76\nEnterprise value = 13.24 + 35.76 = $49.00B\nNet cash: cash $4.594B \u2212 debt $1.518B = +$3.076B [Arithmetic]\nEquity value = $52.08B\nPer share = $52.08B / 321.035M = $162.2\n\nSensitivity (per share) \u2014 WACC / terminal growth:\n| WACC \\ g | 3.0% | 3.5% | 4.0% |\n|----------|------|------|------|\n| 8.0%     | $163 | $173 | $186 |\n| 8.6%     | $151 | $162 | $176 |\n| 9.2%     | $141 | $151 | $163 |\n50% CI: $140\u2013$185 | 80% CI: $110\u2013$210 [Estimate]\n\nStep 3-4 \u2014 Multiples cross-check (no multiple-only conclusion):\n- TTM P/E 34.3x [Aggregator]; 52-week own band 143.39-257.00 [Aggregator]\n  implies P/E 21.7x\u201338.9x on FY2026 EPS $6.61 \u2014 current is at the top decile.\n- Forward P/E: $239.04 / $8.69 guided = 27.5x reported; / $8.09 normalized\n  ex-refund = 29.5x [Arithmetic]\n- EV/EBITDA: EV $73.66B / EBITDA $3.216B (OI $2.707B + D&A $0.509B) = 22.9x\n  [Arithmetic]; 10-year typical range ~12-18x [Assumption \u2014 historical context]\n- P/FCF: 34.8x standard FCF; 37.8x owner FCF [Arithmetic]\n- PEG: 2.9 trailing [Aggregator]\nMultiple-implied fair value at a generous 22-25x normalized FY2027 EPS\n($8.09): $178\u2013$202. This OVERLAPS the DCF top end; even the most generous\nmultiple anchor is below $239.\n\nStep 3-X \u2014 Tornado (top 3 variables):\n1. WACC \u00b10.6% \u2192 \u00b1$11/sh\n2. FY2033 op margin \u00b1100bps \u2192 \u00b1$9/sh\n3. Terminal growth \u00b10.5% \u2192 \u00b1$8/sh\nThesis KPIs set from these: quarterly ex-refund op margin; comps ex-tax-refund\ntiming; tariff policy changes.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3.5-1 \u2014 Competitive response: If Ross sustains +10% comps, TJX ($56B sales)\nand Burlington will compete harder for opportunistic inventory, pressuring\nRoss's gross margin \u22120.5 to \u22121.0 pts over 2-3 years [Assumption]. Impact: \u22121.\nStep 3.5-2 \u2014 Reflexivity: Stock strength has provided strong SBC currency and\nsupplier confidence, but Ross does not rely on equity markets (net cash).\nImpact: minor positive; low relevance [Assumption].\nStep 3.5-3 \u2014 Customer behavior: Current surge is early-adopter trade-down. As\npenetration saturates, the marginal customer is less price-elastic and more\npromiscuous; comps normalize toward low-single-digit [Assumption]. Base case\nalready reflects this.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 4-1/2 \u2014 Scenario distribution (probabilities sum to 100%; base is modal):\n- BEAR (25%): Recession/consumer stress; comps turn negative; tariffs compress\n  margin \u2212150bps; normalized FY2028 EPS $6.50; multiple 16-18x.\n  IV = $100 [Estimate]\n  Trigger evidence: CPI >4%, 10Y >5.2%, comps <0% for two quarters.\n- BASE (50%): Comps fade to 3-4%, store growth 5-6%/yr, op margin 14% by FY2029,\n  WACC 8.6%, terminal 3.5%. IV = $162 [DCF]\n- BULL (25%): Off-price \"golden age\" persists; comps 5-7%; op margin 15%;\n  revenue $38B by FY2033; WACC 8.2%; IV = $195 [Estimate]\nStep 4-3 \u2014 Bayesian update: Prior (base-rate): probability of outperformance at\n30x+ multiple = ~25%. Likelihood from Q2 FY2027 evidence (+10% comps, +205bps\nex-refund margin, guidance raised): strongly positive \u2192 Posterior: Bear 25% /\nBase 50% / Bull 25%. Note the market's implied posterior (per Section 1.5) is\nmore aggressive than my posterior.\nStep 4-4/5 \u2014 Expected price and MoS:\nExpected price = 0.25\u00d7100 + 0.50\u00d7162 + 0.25\u00d7195 = $155.75 [Arithmetic]\n50% CI: $140\u2013$185 | 80% CI: $110\u2013$210\nMargin of safety = ($155.75 \u2212 $239.04) / $155.75 = \u221253.5%; base-case MoS =\n(162\u2212239)/162 = \u221247.5%. Judgment: NONE (deeply negative).\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: To earn 9%/yr from $239, price in 5 years \u2248 $368. At a\n20-25x exit multiple, FY2031 EPS must be $14.7\u2013$18.4, implying a 5-yr EPS CAGR\nof 12.7\u201317.8% from $8.09 normalized [Arithmetic].\nMy horizon: 3 years at high-single-digit growth, then mid-single-digit\n(5-yr normalized EPS CAGR \u2248 8-9%).\nSpread = My horizon value \u2212 Market horizon value \u2192 NEGATIVE, no arbitrage.\nThe market is discounting a materially higher growth trajectory than the store\nmath supports (2,900 + 700 long-run cap).\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 5-1 \u2014 Conviction score (max 15):\n- Business quality: 3/3 (wide moat, fortress balance sheet, 13/15 allocation)\n- Valuation attractiveness: 0/3 (price 47% above base IV; MoS negative)\n- Macro alignment: 2/3 (trade-down tailwind vs. rate risk = neutral)\n- Thesis clarity: 3/3 (comps ex-refunds, op margin, store count are measurable)\n- Execution traceability: 3/3 (6/8 beats; guidance raised twice)\nRaw score: 11/15 \u2192 rubric \"High\". Step 9-2 logic-combat gap <2 and negative MoS\ntrigger a one-level cap \u2192 FINAL CONVICTION: MEDIUM (effective 8/15).\nStep 5-2 \u2014 Conviction decay: \u22121 per 6 months without confirming evidence\napplies to any existing position; no new position is justified.\nStep 5-3 \u2014 Sizing: Medium conviction + negative MoS \u2192 0% new money; existing\nholders cap at \u22643% (framework base 5-8% reduced 2pp for negative MoS and 1pp\nfor crowding).\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- Short interest: 3.53% of float; 9.74M shares; DTC 4.17 days; MoM \u22129.7%\n  [Aggregator \u2014 shorts covering, NO squeeze setup]\n- Institutional ownership: 95.29%; insider 2.13% [Aggregator \u2014 no insider\n  cluster-buy signal; insider holding low]\n- Options positioning: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved]\n- Sentiment: strongly bullish \u2014 stock near 52-wk high $257, guidance raised,\n  analyst mean $255.44 > price [Aggregator]. Contrarian caution warranted.\n- Crowding risk: 9/15 (index-heavy, style-crowded quality/momentum names are\n  vulnerable if rates reprice). Position cap \u22121pp.\n- One-time EPS distortion: reported FY2027 EPS includes ~$0.60/sh refund\n  (~7% of guided earnings) [Estimate \u2014 company via Motley Fool]; the market\n  is partially paying for a non-repeatable item.\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nROST offers defensive growth with counter-cyclical demand, a net-cash balance\nsheet and low correlation to pure discretionary. However, at this price it is\nprimarily a momentum/crowding trade, not an investment. If the portfolio\nalready holds TJX, WMT or consumer-defensive proxies, ROST adds redundancy,\nnot diversification. Priority for new capital at T0 should go to assets with\npositive expected returns; ROST fails that test.\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nExpected return = $155.75/$239.04 \u2212 1 = \u221234.8% [Arithmetic]\nKelly fraction = negative \u2192 0% position.\nBreak-even price for a positive Kelly at scenario-weighted IV of $156: ~$125.\nPrice at which Kelly would support a ~6-8% weight: \u2264$120. T0 is ~2\u00d7 the\neconomically justified entry zone.\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nCatalysts:\n- Further tariff-refund receipts (additional litigation coverage) [Unconfirmed]\n- Sustained trade-down into a 2026-27 slowdown (reflation\u2192recession shift) \u2192 +5-10% IV\n- Execution of 115 stores/yr toward 2,900 Ross + 700 dd's [Actual \u2014 company target]\n- Margin expansion to 14%+ via buying leverage \u2192 +8-10% IV\nRisks:\n- New broad-based tariffs (not refunded) \u2192 \u2212150-300bps gross margin \u2192 Bear case\n- Comps mean-reversion in H2 FY2027 (tough laps) \u2192 multiple de-rating\n- Consumer recovery ends trade-down; mid/low-income wage growth strengthens\n- TJX/Burlington aggressive sourcing compresses buying advantage\n- Valuation: 34x TTM P/E is at the top of its own 52-week band; any EPS miss\n  risks a 30-40% correction.\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nTail triggers shared with the portfolio: (1) rates repricing (10Y >5.2%) \u2014\ncompresses ROST's multiple more than most staples; (2) inflation re-acceleration\n\u2014 margin risk; (3) recession \u2014 near-term demand risk despite trade-down.\nStress correlation to XLP/XLY estimated 0.6-0.7 [Assumption].\nConditional VaR (5%): P/E from 34x to 18x on normalized EPS $8.09 \u2192 ~$146,\nplus earnings drawdown to ~$7.00 \u2192 ~$126; 5% CVaR \u2248 \u221245 to \u221250% from T0\n[Estimate]. ROST is not tail-hedged at this price.\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nThesis integrity triggers (miss any two \u2192 full re-evaluation):\n1. Quarterly comps EXCLUDING tax-refund/tariff timing: <1% for two consecutive\n   quarters.\n2. Operating margin ex-refund: <12.5% from tariffs/wage pressure.\n3. Gross margin: >100bps year-over-year decline not attributable to refunds.\n4. Store openings <90/yr.\n5. Inventory growth > sales growth by >5pts for two quarters (markdown risk).\n6. Net cash < $1.5B (leverage build).\n7. 10Y >5.5% or HY spread >4.5% \u2014 macro regime overlay triggers de-rating review.\nPre-mortem early-warning indicators: Q2 FY2027's 405bps refund benefit must not\nbe treated as recurring; if sell-side begins capitalizing it into \"core EPS,\"\nthat is an exit signal, not a validation.\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM \u2014 SHORT THESIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1. FY2027 reported EPS growth of +23-30% is inflated ~7pts by a one-time tariff\n   refund (~$0.60 of $8.69 guidance) [Estimate]; underlying growth is high\n   single-digit, not \"exceptional.\"\n2. At 27.5x forward reported / 29.5x normalized P/E and 22.9x EV/EBITDA, the\n   stock prices in a decade of double-digit growth for a 2,000-store US chain\n   with a stated ~3,600-store ceiling.\n3. Trade-down is cyclical: when the low/mid-income consumer recovers, comps\n   mean-revert \u2014 2024-25 comps were \u22121.8%, +2.6%, +4.6%, +10.4%, +12.2%, +20.6%\n   [Filed]: the recent double-digits are the anomaly, not the base rate.\n4. New tariffs are asymmetric risk: the refund was a one-time litigation\n   outcome; a fresh tariff cycle without refunds compresses gross margin.\n5. TJX is 2.5\u00d7 Ross's scale and Burlington is growing faster; buying\n   advantages can narrow.\n6. Buybacks at 30x+ earnings are value-destructive; capital returns partially\n   recycle SBC dilution rather than creating net shareholder value.\n7. Technicals: 95% institutional ownership, near 52-wk high, crowded quality\n   \u2014 a single guidance miss de-rates 34x \u2192 22x = ~$145 (\u221239%).\n\nLogic combat (Long vs Short): Long logic 7/10, Short logic 6/10.\nGap = 1 < 2 \u2192 CONVICTION DOWNGRADED one level (as recorded in Section 5).\nKilled arguments: \"Tariffs help off-price\" \u2014 survives ONLY as a differential\nbenefit, not a uniform tariff regime; dead if tariffs are broad-based.\n\"Expansion to 3,600 stores justifies the multiple\" \u2014 store growth is ~5%/yr\nunit math; comps carry the rest and are mean-reverting. \"Quality deserves a\npremium\" \u2014 yes, but the premium embedded is a 10-year double-digit growth\nassumption, not a quality premium.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nA. Methodology: PASS \u2014 Engine 1 justified; no DCF-only or multiple-only\n   conclusion; no TAM narrative used.\nB. Valuation: PASS \u2014 WACC 8.6% (sourced rf 4.70% [Actual], beta 0.877\n   [Aggregator], ERP 4.5% [Assumption]); explicit 7-yr path; terminal 3.5% \u2264\n   nominal GDP; sensitivity shown. Conclusion (price vs IV gap) is robust: even\n   at 8.0% WACC and 15% margin