y sensitivity: 0 \u2014 Ross does not need capital markets; consumer access to\n  credit is a mild factor.\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: +2 \u2014 off-price is counter-cyclical: trade-down demand\n  accelerates in weak economies.\n- Commodity sensitivity: -1 \u2014 oil $87 raises freight/logistics costs.\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: Regime-weighted impact = +0.35\u00d7(+1) + 0.40\u00d7(+2) + 0.15\u00d7(+2)\n+ 0.10\u00d7(+1) \u2248 +1.3 \u2192 TAILWIND for Ross's demand, partially offset by import-cost push.\nConviction +0.5 step. Note: macro alignment does NOT change intrinsic value; the\nvaluation gap below is a price/expectations gap, not a demand gap.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates for this archetype:\n- Large-cap US off-price retailer 5-year survival: ~95% (TJX, ROST, BURL all\n  independent for 20+ years) [Estimate]\n- Wide-moat consumer-discretionary compounder growing earnings over 5 years: ~75-80%\n  [Estimate]\n- Top-2 off-price retailer continuing to gain share: ~50-60% [Estimate]\n- Sustained 10%+ annual growth for a decade at $25B revenue scale: ~20-25% [Estimate]\n- Big-box retailer falling >40% within 3 years of a cyclical peak: ~30-40% [Estimate]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate: Base-case growth (5-6% annually after FY2027) is\nIN LINE with base rates; bull-case (8-10% sustained) is modestly above. Justification\nfor any deviation: (1) off-price sector's secular share gains vs full-price retail;\n(2) fortress balance sheet funds ~115 stores/yr without leverage; (3) supply glut from\ndepartment-store order cancellations deepens Ross's buying advantage.\nStep 1.5-3. Shock check: Bull probability 30% vs base-rate 22.5% for a decade of 10%\ngrowth \u2192 ratio 1.33x, below the 1.5x red flag. PASS.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------